From: Angel Martinez <110217098+anmartinez1256@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 01:51:09 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 19/39] UPML: the scalar SSE path four y-rows at a time

A PML box that is not a full-z slab (the SMA launch's PML_8 at z-max) ran the scalar SSE path: per cell
and component a lane offset, a table index, two coefficients, the flux and the field value, 13
instructions in the inner loop. With the coefficient table, four consecutive y-rows of an x-line are now
one group: the fluxes and the distinct coefficient sets are stored per group (lane = row), so a group
takes one vector operation per step of the update while the field values are gathered and scattered lane
by lane (about 7 instructions per cell). When the box is not a multiple of four rows wide, the last group
of an x-line runs lane by lane on the same arrays; the rows are handed to the threads in whole groups.
Same operations per cell as the scalar path (bit-identical); OPENEMS_PML_YVEC=0 keeps the scalar rows.

Verified bit-identical against the template hooks (basic engine) on boxes with PML_8 on all faces and
mixed with Mur, lumped elements and conducting sheets, 37, 38 and 39 y lines, at 1, 3 and 4 threads,
with the fused sweep and without it (Debye block), and on the six benchmark models against stock
(bench/check.sh). SMA launch, paired interleaved A/B, 6 rounds (other processes on 0.4-0.7 cores):
x1.183 at 1 thread (421 -> 498 MC/s), x1.125 at 4 (1256 -> 1403), x1.129 at 8 (1303 -> 1471).

